#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e83b69f4-4396-5458-961d-379c85eb8536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350639} {:answer/id #uuid "f77e869d-ba44-56db-923a-6488b4c4df83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350640} {:answer/id #uuid "9e58365b-0782-5893-b776-dba3a1fc926b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350641} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fa8594-4785-53d0-92d0-5a0f78e75574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350642} {:answer/id #uuid "654336de-2d6d-5292-bad6-f9e7b54fdcaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e58365b-0782-5893-b776-dba3a1fc926b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350641} :answer-field/id #uuid "1060da2b-be12-5fc6-9f02-223cf08e1535" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ba67b8a-fb87-555b-9bbd-2816c5ef8c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-125460" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{5}{y}\\,i-\\frac{5}{x}\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Interpret circulation as the sum of the four convergent improper line integrals over the open quadrant arcs, taking independent one-sided limits at $(0,\\pm1)$ and $(\\pm1,0)$. Equivalently, integrate the continuous extension of the scalar pullback $F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)$. The field itself remains undefined at the four axis intersections, so the original ordinary circulation is undefined." :question/worked-solution "The field is undefined where $x=0$ or $y=0$, and the unit circle passes through four such points. Thus the original ordinary circulation is undefined. Under the stated improper-integral convention, the given parameterization is clockwise and\n\n$$r(t)=(\\sin t,\\cos t),\\qquad r\\prime(t)=(\\cos t,-\\sin t).$$\n\nOn each open quadrant arc, both coordinates are nonzero, so\n\n$$F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)=\\frac{5}{\\cos t}\\cos t+\\left(-\\frac{5}{\\sin t}\\right)(-\\sin t)=10.$$\n\nEvery one-sided endpoint limit of this scalar pullback is $10$. Each arc integral converges absolutely, independently of the other arcs. With $a_k=k\\pi/2$,\n\n$$\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{imp}}=\\sum_{k=0}^{3}\\lim_{\\epsilon_k\\downarrow0,\\,\\delta_k\\downarrow0}\\int_{a_k+\\epsilon_k}^{a_{k+1}-\\delta_k}10\\,dt=4(5\\pi)=20\\pi.$$\n\nThis also equals the integral of the scalar pullback extended continuously by $10$ at its missing points. Only the scalar pullback is extended; the vector field is not assigned finite limits there. No principal-value cancellation or application of Green's theorem is needed."}]}
